  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The model's performance and the reported 7% to 14% efficiency gains are demonstrated solely on a single case study in North Florida.",
      "what": "The study develops a mathematical model for the multi-period inventory pickup and delivery problem in rural logistics, incorporating alternative routes characterized by varying costs, accident risks, and toll fees. The model was tested using a tailored non-dominated sorting genetic algorithm II (NSGA-II) on a mixed urban-rural distribution network in North Florida, achieving a 7% to 14% reduction in both transportation costs and accident risks.",
      "why": "It introduces a routing optimization approach that simultaneously manages inventory over multiple periods and integrates return-trip pickups, allowing logistics planners to systematically trade off financial costs against safety risks on rural road networks."
    }
